Ming-Image: encode a prompt once for many seeds, and let a second prompt work.

The conditioning encoder is a ~16B MoE (about 10 GB at 4 bits). The pipeline
evicts it after it runs so the DiT has the memory to itself. Upstream that
has two costs:
- N pictures of one prompt take N runs, and each one loads and runs the
  encoder again. `num_images` batches them instead, which multiplies the
  DiT's memory. It also draws every picture from ONE noise key, so seed k
  alone never gives the picture seed k gave in the batch.
- A second `generate_array` call on the same pipeline (a server, a notebook)
  fails with `'NoneType' object has no attribute 'encode'`: the encoder was
  evicted and nothing loads it back.

This patch:
- `encode(prompt)` returns the two small conditioning arrays, and
  `generate_array(..., conditioning=...)` takes them.
- `generate_seeds(prompt, seeds)` yields `(seed, image)` per seed after ONE
  encode. Each image is exactly what that seed gives on its own (its own
  noise key).
- `_encode` loads an evicted encoder back, dropping the DiT and VAE first so
  the encoder never sits beside them. They load again after it, as on a
  first run.
- The argument checks move into `_validate`, so `generate_seeds` rejects a
  bad size before paying for the encode.
Upstreamable.

